1. DATA LIFECYCLE DESIGN PRINCIPLE
OneDraft shall treat project data as a controlled lifecycle rather than as undifferentiated application storage.
Every significant project artifact, model state, regulatory source, validation result, drawing, document and acceptance record shall possess identifiable provenance.
The system shall distinguish:
Active Data
Current operational project information.
Historical Data
Previous project, model, revision and configuration states.
Derived Data
Geometry, drawings, schedules, validation outputs and document packages generated from authoritative project state.
Immutable Evidence
Hashes, audit events, acceptance records, regulatory manifests and other records whose historical integrity must be preserved.
Archived Data
Data retained for historical, contractual, regulatory or operational purposes but removed from active working paths.
Deleted Data
Data that has passed its applicable retention requirements and is authorized for destruction.
The lifecycle system therefore governs:
CREATE
 ↓
ACTIVE
 ↓
VERSIONED
 ↓
HISTORICAL
 ↓
ARCHIVED
 ↓
RETENTION EXPIRY
 ↓
AUTHORIZED DELETION

2. DATA OWNERSHIP PRINCIPLE
Every persistent OneDraft object shall have an identifiable ownership context.
The minimum ownership hierarchy is:
PLATFORM
 ↓
ORGANIZATION
 ↓
PROJECT
 ↓
OBJECT
 ↓
REVISION
 ↓
DERIVED ARTIFACT
Ownership determines:
access
retention
archival
deletion authority
provenance
audit requirements
No artifact may become orphaned from its project ownership context.

3. DATA CLASSIFICATION
OneDraft shall classify persistent data according to operational significance.
Initial classifications:
PROJECT_DATA
MODEL_DATA
GEOMETRY_DATA
COMPLIANCE_DATA
VALIDATION_DATA
DOCUMENTATION_DATA
AUDIT_DATA
IDENTITY_DATA
BILLING_DATA
SYSTEM_DATA
ARTIFACT_DATA
Classification shall be stored through explicit metadata where required.

4. DATA SENSITIVITY
The lifecycle system shall support sensitivity classifications.
Initial values:
PUBLIC
INTERNAL
CONFIDENTIAL
RESTRICTED
SYSTEM
Sensitivity classification does not replace authorization.
Authorization remains governed by the Identity, Access Control & Security System.

5. DATA RECORD METADATA
Persistent records should support lifecycle metadata where applicable:
id
created_at
updated_at
created_by
updated_by
organization_id
project_id
status
data_classification
retention_policy_id
archived_at
deleted_at
Not every table requires every field.
The implementation shall avoid unnecessary duplication where ownership can be derived through relational relationships.

6. LIFECYCLE STATES
Standard lifecycle states shall include:
DRAFT
ACTIVE
SUPERSEDED
HISTORICAL
ARCHIVED
LOCKED
PENDING_DELETION
DELETED
Certain subsystems may define additional domain-specific states.
Lifecycle state transitions must be controlled by application services rather than unrestricted database mutation.

7. STATE TRANSITION RULE
A lifecycle transition shall be represented conceptually as:
CURRENT STATE
 ↓
VALIDATE TRANSITION
 ↓
AUTHORIZE
 ↓
RECORD EVENT
 ↓
UPDATE STATE
 ↓
COMMIT
Invalid transitions shall be rejected.
For example:
DELETED → ACTIVE
shall not be permitted through ordinary application operations.

8. PROJECT DATA LIFECYCLE
A project shall progress through:
INTAKE
 ↓
SPECIFICATION
 ↓
MODELING
 ↓
VALIDATION
 ↓
DOCUMENTATION
 ↓
REVIEW
 ↓
ACCEPTANCE
 ↓
FINALIZED
 ↓
ARCHIVED
The lifecycle system shall preserve the relationship between project state and the underlying model/revision state.

9. MODEL DATA LIFECYCLE
The computational project model shall be versioned.
Conceptually:
MODEL VERSION 1
 ↓
MODEL VERSION 2
 ↓
MODEL VERSION 3
 ↓
MODEL VERSION N
A newer model version does not destroy the previous version.
Previous versions become historical project state.

10. REVISION RETENTION
Every finalized revision shall remain reconstructable for the applicable retention period.
A revision shall retain references to:
project_id
model_version_id
parent_revision_id
code_manifest_id
validation_results
drawing_set
document_package
change_records
acceptance_records
This allows the complete revision state to be reconstructed.

11. DERIVED DATA PROVENANCE
Derived artifacts shall identify the authoritative state from which they were generated.
A drawing shall identify:
project_id
revision_id
model_version_id
view_id
A validation result shall identify:
project_id
revision_id
model_version_id
code_manifest_id
validation_run_id
A document package shall identify:
project_id
revision_id
model_version_id
code_manifest_id
validation_run_id

12. PROVENANCE CHAIN
The minimum provenance chain shall be:
PROJECT
 ↓
MODEL VERSION
 ↓
REVISION
 ↓
CODE MANIFEST
 ↓
VALIDATION RUN
 ↓
DRAWING SET
 ↓
DOCUMENT PACKAGE
 ↓
ACCEPTANCE
Each derived stage shall be traceable backward to its authoritative source.

13. ARTIFACT PROVENANCE
Every stored artifact shall maintain provenance metadata.
Conceptually:
artifact_id
project_id
revision_id
model_version_id
artifact_type
source_system
generation_job_id
content_hash
created_at
created_by
Artifacts lacking sufficient provenance shall not be classified as final project deliverables.

14. CONTENT HASHING
Important persistent artifacts shall receive cryptographic content hashes.
The initial implementation shall support:
SHA-256
The hash shall represent the stored artifact content.
Example:
document.pdf
 ↓
SHA-256
 ↓
document_hash
The hash provides an integrity identifier rather than a substitute for access control.

15. MODEL STATE HASHING
Model versions shall contain a deterministic state hash.
Conceptually:
MODEL OBJECTS
+
RELATIONSHIPS
+
CONSTRAINTS
+
PARAMETERS
 ↓
CANONICAL SERIALIZATION
 ↓
HASH
 ↓
STATE_HASH
The canonicalization process must be deterministic.
Equivalent model states must produce the same canonical representation before hashing.

16. REVISION HASHING
Where required, a revision may additionally maintain a deterministic revision hash.
The revision hash shall incorporate the authoritative model state and revision metadata necessary to identify that revision.
This allows later verification that a reconstructed revision corresponds to the originally recorded state.

17. ARTIFACT STORAGE
Large binary artifacts shall not normally be stored directly inside PostgreSQL.
The architecture shall use object storage for:
PDF
DWG
DXF
IFC
SVG
PNG
JPEG
ZIP
CAD exports
generated document packages
source documents
large geometry payloads
PostgreSQL stores metadata and object-storage references.

18. ARTIFACT REFERENCE
The artifact record shall conceptually contain:
id UUID
project_id UUID
revision_id UUID
artifact_type VARCHAR(64)
storage_provider VARCHAR(64)
storage_bucket VARCHAR(255)
storage_key TEXT
content_hash VARCHAR(128)
content_size BIGINT
mime_type VARCHAR(255)
created_at TIMESTAMPTZ
The exact physical implementation may vary by deployment environment.

19. OBJECT STORAGE INTEGRITY
An artifact shall not be considered successfully stored until:
OBJECT UPLOAD
 ↓
OBJECT VERIFICATION
 ↓
HASH VERIFICATION
 ↓
DATABASE REFERENCE
 ↓
COMMIT
has completed successfully.
A database reference to a nonexistent or unverified artifact shall not become authoritative.

20. SOURCE DOCUMENTS
Imported source documents shall preserve their original identity and provenance.
Examples:
SURVEY
CODE DOCUMENT
OWNER SPECIFICATION
CONSULTANT DOCUMENT
EXISTING CAD
BIM MODEL
REFERENCE IMAGE
PRODUCT DATA
PROJECT BRIEF
The original source artifact shall remain distinct from any parsed or transformed representation.

21. SOURCE DOCUMENT HASH
Every imported source document should receive:
content_hash
content_size
mime_type
source_reference
import_timestamp
imported_by
If the same source document is imported repeatedly, the content hash can be used to detect duplicates.

22. PARSED DATA PROVENANCE
When information is extracted from a source document, the resulting structured data shall retain a reference to the source.
Conceptually:
SOURCE DOCUMENT
 ↓
EXTRACTION
 ↓
STRUCTURED PROJECT DATA
The structured data shall identify the source artifact where practical.
This prevents extracted information from becoming detached from its originating document.

23. AI-GENERATED DATA PROVENANCE
AI-generated project modifications shall identify the originating AI operation.
Conceptually:
USER REQUEST
 ↓
ARIA COMMAND
 ↓
MODEL OPERATION
 ↓
REVISION
The resulting revision shall therefore be traceable to the originating command.
Where applicable, the system shall retain:
command_id
request_id
model/provider metadata
command parameters
validation requirements
execution result
Provider-specific information shall remain behind the AI provider abstraction.

24. HUMAN-GENERATED DATA PROVENANCE
Human-authored changes shall also be recorded.
Examples include:
MANUAL EDIT
REDLINE
APPROVAL
REJECTION
ACCEPTANCE
COMMENT
REVIEW
OVERRIDE
The system shall distinguish the originating actor from the operation itself.

25. MACHINE-GENERATED DATA PROVENANCE
Automated workers shall produce identifiable execution metadata.
Examples:
worker_id
job_id
execution_id
service_name
software_version
created_at
This permits a generated artifact to be traced to the execution environment that produced it.

26. PROVENANCE GRAPH
OneDraft shall conceptually maintain a provenance graph:
SOURCE
   ↓
REQUIREMENT
   ↓
MODEL
   ↓
COMMAND
   ↓
REVISION
   ↓
VALIDATION
   ↓
DRAWING
   ↓
DOCUMENT
   ↓
ACCEPTANCE
The graph may be represented through existing relational references rather than requiring a dedicated graph database in the MVP.

27. REQUIREMENT PROVENANCE
Each project requirement shall identify its originating source.
Possible sources:
CUSTOMER
JURISDICTION
CODE
CONSULTANT
PROJECT_STANDARD
ARIA
SYSTEM
Requirements shall remain traceable to the source that created them.

28. COMPLIANCE PROVENANCE
Regulatory validation shall identify the code source and manifest used.
The provenance chain shall include:
CODE SOURCE
 ↓
CODE RULE
 ↓
CODE MANIFEST
 ↓
VALIDATION RUN
 ↓
VALIDATION RESULT
A finalized revision shall therefore preserve the regulatory basis used during validation.

29. IMMUTABLE REGULATORY MANIFESTS
Once a code manifest has been attached to a finalized revision, it shall become immutable.
If the applicable regulatory source changes, OneDraft shall create a new manifest rather than modifying the previous one.
Conceptually:
MANIFEST 1
 ↓
FINALIZED REVISION

MANIFEST 2
 ↓
NEW REVISION
Historical regulatory context must remain reconstructable.

30. VALIDATION PROVENANCE
Every validation result shall identify:
validation_run_id
revision_id
model_version_id
code_manifest_id
rule_id
affected_objects
execution_timestamp
This establishes which project state was evaluated against which regulatory rules.

31. DOCUMENT PROVENANCE
A final document package shall never exist solely as an unqualified file.
The package must be associated with:
PROJECT
REVISION
MODEL VERSION
CODE MANIFEST
VALIDATION RUN
GENERATION JOB
DOCUMENT HASH
This makes the document package reproducible and auditable.

32. ACCEPTANCE PROVENANCE
Customer acceptance shall identify:
customer_id
project_id
revision_id
model_version_id
document_package_id
accepted_at
acceptance_status
Acceptance therefore applies to a defined project state rather than to an unspecified collection of files.

33. AUDIT PROVENANCE
All material lifecycle transitions shall produce audit events.
Examples:
PROJECT_CREATED
MODEL_VERSION_CREATED
REVISION_CREATED
COMMAND_EXECUTED
VALIDATION_COMPLETED
DRAWING_GENERATED
DOCUMENT_GENERATED
ARTIFACT_STORED
ARTIFACT_ARCHIVED
ACCEPTANCE_RECORDED
DATA_DELETED
The audit event shall identify the actor and affected project/object where applicable.

34. RETENTION POLICY
Retention shall be policy-driven.
A retention policy shall conceptually define:
policy_id
data_classification
retention_period
archive_after
deletion_after
legal_hold_behavior
status
Retention periods shall not be hard-coded throughout application services.

35. RETENTION POLICY ASSOCIATION
Data objects shall resolve their retention policy through classification and ownership.
Conceptually:
OBJECT
 ↓
DATA CLASSIFICATION
 ↓
RETENTION POLICY
 ↓
LIFECYCLE ACTION
This permits retention requirements to evolve without rewriting every storage subsystem.

36. RETENTION CLOCK
Retention timing shall be based on an explicit lifecycle event.
Possible anchors include:
CREATION
FINALIZATION
ACCEPTANCE
PROJECT CLOSURE
LAST ACTIVITY
ARCHIVAL
The applicable anchor must be defined by the relevant retention policy.

37. ARCHIVAL
Archival shall move data from active operational storage into controlled historical storage.
Conceptually:
ACTIVE DATA
 ↓
ARCHIVE ELIGIBILITY
 ↓
ARCHIVAL JOB
 ↓
INTEGRITY CHECK
 ↓
ARCHIVED
Archival shall preserve provenance.

38. ARCHIVE MANIFEST
An archival operation should create an archive manifest containing:
archive_id
project_id
objects
artifact_references
content_hashes
source_versions
created_at
archived_at
archive_format
The manifest permits later verification of the archived dataset.

39. ARCHIVE INTEGRITY
Archived data shall remain verifiable.
The system should periodically verify:
stored object exists
content hash matches
metadata remains consistent
provenance references remain resolvable
Failures shall generate operational alerts.

40. PROJECT ARCHIVAL
A project may become eligible for archival after completion of its active lifecycle.
Conceptually:
ACCEPTED
 ↓
FINALIZED
 ↓
INACTIVE
 ↓
ARCHIVE ELIGIBLE
 ↓
ARCHIVED
Archival shall not destroy the project's historical state.

41. LEGAL OR ADMINISTRATIVE HOLD
The lifecycle system shall support a hold mechanism.
A held object shall not be automatically deleted while the hold remains active.
Conceptually:
RETENTION EXPIRY
+
ACTIVE HOLD
=
NO DELETION
A hold should identify:
hold_id
scope
reason
created_by
created_at
released_by
released_at

42. DELETION ELIGIBILITY
An object becomes deletion-eligible only when:
RETENTION EXPIRED
AND
NO ACTIVE HOLD
AND
NO REQUIRED DEPENDENCY
AND
AUTHORIZED DELETION POLICY EXISTS
Eligibility does not itself execute deletion.

43. DELETION AUTHORIZATION
Deletion shall pass through an explicit application operation.
Conceptually:
DELETION CANDIDATE
 ↓
POLICY CHECK
 ↓
DEPENDENCY CHECK
 ↓
HOLD CHECK
 ↓
AUTHORIZATION
 ↓
DELETION JOB
The operation shall be auditable.

44. SOFT DELETION
User-facing objects should initially use soft deletion.
Conceptually:
status = DELETED
deleted_at = timestamp
The object remains available for controlled recovery or historical reference until permanent deletion is authorized.

45. HARD DELETION
Permanent deletion shall be reserved for data that has satisfied all applicable deletion conditions.
Hard deletion may include:
database record removal
object-storage deletion
derived-cache deletion
search-index removal
secondary replica expiration
The system must account for all storage locations.

46. CASCADE DELETION CONTROL
OneDraft shall avoid uncontrolled database cascade deletion for historically significant project records.
Relationships involving:
revisions
model_versions
validation_results
document_packages
acceptance_records
audit_events
shall receive explicit lifecycle treatment.
The objective is to prevent accidental destruction of provenance.

47. DEPENDENCY CHECKING
Before deleting an object, the system shall determine whether other authoritative or historical objects depend upon it.
Example:
MODEL VERSION
 ↓
REVISION
 ↓
DRAWING
 ↓
DOCUMENT PACKAGE
 ↓
ACCEPTANCE
A model version supporting a finalized document package shall not be silently deleted.

48. DERIVED DATA REGENERATION
Where a derived artifact can be regenerated from authoritative state, the system may prioritize preservation of the authoritative source over indefinite retention of every generated derivative.
For example:
PROJECT MODEL
+
VIEW
+
DRAWING RULES
 ↓
DRAWING
However, finalized deliverables may still require independent retention according to contractual or regulatory policy.

49. CACHE DATA
Temporary computational data may have shorter retention.
Examples:
geometry cache
render cache
temporary previews
worker scratch files
temporary exports
job intermediates
These records may be automatically expired after successful completion unless required for debugging, audit or reproducibility.

50. JOB ARTIFACT RETENTION
Worker jobs shall distinguish:
temporary artifact
intermediate artifact
final artifact
failed-job artifact
diagnostic artifact
Temporary artifacts may be automatically removed after a defined period.
Final artifacts remain governed by project retention policy.

51. EVENT RETENTION
Event records shall receive explicit retention classification.
Material project events should remain available for the applicable historical period.
Operational telemetry events may use shorter retention.
This distinction prevents high-volume infrastructure telemetry from being confused with authoritative project audit history.

52. AUDIT EVENT IMMUTABILITY
Authoritative audit events shall be append-only.
Existing events shall not normally be updated.
Corrections shall be represented by additional events.
Conceptually:
ORIGINAL EVENT
 ↓
CORRECTION EVENT
rather than:
UPDATE ORIGINAL EVENT

53. PROVENANCE IMMUTABILITY
The following records shall become immutable when finalized:
FINALIZED MODEL VERSION
FINALIZED REVISION
FINALIZED CODE MANIFEST
FINAL VALIDATION RUN
FINAL DOCUMENT PACKAGE
CUSTOMER ACCEPTANCE
Any subsequent change produces a new version or revision.

54. FINALIZATION BOUNDARY
Finalization shall create a controlled boundary between mutable working data and historical project state.
Conceptually:
WORKING MODEL
 ↓
VALIDATION
 ↓
REVIEW
 ↓
FINALIZATION
 ↓
IMMUTABLE PROJECT STATE
The finalization operation shall be recorded in the audit system.

55. RECONSTRUCTION REQUIREMENT
The lifecycle architecture shall support reconstruction of a historical project state.
Given:
project_id
revision_id
model_version_id
code_manifest_id
the system must be able to identify:
authoritative model state
applicable requirements
applicable constraints
regulatory basis
validation results
drawing references
document package
acceptance state

56. PROVENANCE VERIFICATION
OneDraft shall provide an internal verification process.
Conceptually:
SELECT HISTORICAL REVISION
 ↓
RESOLVE MODEL
 ↓
RESOLVE CODE MANIFEST
 ↓
RESOLVE VALIDATION
 ↓
RESOLVE DRAWINGS
 ↓
RESOLVE DOCUMENT PACKAGE
 ↓
VERIFY HASHES
 ↓
REPORT RESULT
The verification result shall identify any missing or inconsistent provenance.

57. DATA INTEGRITY CHECK
The lifecycle system shall periodically check for:
orphaned records
missing artifacts
hash mismatches
broken references
invalid lifecycle states
invalid ownership
missing provenance
inconsistent revision references
Integrity failures shall be surfaced through the Observability and System Health subsystem.

58. DATA LINEAGE
Lineage shall describe how information moved through the system.
Example:
OWNER SPECIFICATION
 ↓
PROJECT REQUIREMENT
 ↓
ARIA COMMAND
 ↓
MODEL CHANGE
 ↓
REVISION
 ↓
DRAWING
 ↓
DOCUMENT
Lineage is distinct from audit history.
Audit answers:
What happened?
Lineage answers:
Where did this information come from?

59. SOURCE-TO-OUTPUT TRACEABILITY
OneDraft should eventually support a traceability query such as:
Which project requirement caused this drawing annotation?
The system shall traverse:
REQUIREMENT
 ↓
AFFECTED OBJECT
 ↓
REVISION
 ↓
VIEW
 ↓
ANNOTATION
This becomes important for complex projects and regulatory review.

60. OUTPUT-TO-SOURCE TRACEABILITY
The reverse query shall also be supported:
Which drawings and documents were affected by this requirement?
The dependency graph shall identify downstream outputs.

61. DATA LINEAGE API
The future API shall support a provenance endpoint conceptually similar to:
GET /api/v1/projects/{project_id}/provenance
Possible query parameters:
revision_id
object_id
artifact_id
direction
depth
Possible directions:
UPSTREAM
DOWNSTREAM
BOTH

62. ARTIFACT HISTORY API
The API shall support historical artifact inspection.
Conceptually:
GET /api/v1/projects/{project_id}/artifacts
Filters may include:
artifact_type
revision_id
created_after
created_before
status
hash

63. RETENTION MANAGEMENT API
Administrative services may expose:
GET /api/v1/admin/retention/policies
GET /api/v1/admin/retention/candidates
POST /api/v1/admin/retention/archive
POST /api/v1/admin/retention/delete
These operations require administrative authorization.

64. DATA LIFECYCLE JOBS
Long-running lifecycle operations shall use the Job & Worker Execution System.
Examples:
ARCHIVE_PROJECT
VERIFY_ARCHIVE
DELETE_EXPIRED_DATA
VERIFY_ARTIFACT_INTEGRITY
REBUILD_PROVENANCE
RECONSTRUCT_REVISION
The lifecycle system shall therefore integrate with the existing worker architecture rather than creating an independent job system.

65. EVENT BUS INTEGRATION
Lifecycle events shall be published through the Integration & Event Bus.
Examples:
artifact.created
artifact.archived
artifact.deleted
revision.finalized
project.archived
retention.expired
deletion.completed
provenance.verified
integrity.failure
Consumers may include:
audit service
observability service
notification service
administrative control plane
backup service

66. BACKUP INTEGRATION
The lifecycle system shall cooperate with the Backup, Disaster Recovery & Business Continuity subsystem.
Retention and backup are distinct controls.
Deletion from active storage does not automatically imply immediate destruction from every backup copy.
Backup expiration shall therefore follow the separate backup retention policy.

67. BACKUP / DELETION DISTINCTION
The architecture shall distinguish:
APPLICATION DELETION
from:
BACKUP EXPIRATION
An object deleted from the active system may remain temporarily within retained backups according to the applicable recovery policy.

68. MULTI-TENANT DATA LIFECYCLE
All lifecycle operations must respect organization boundaries.
Conceptually:
ORGANIZATION
 ↓
PROJECT
 ↓
DATA
An administrator for one organization must never be able to archive, export or delete another organization's project data through ordinary tenant-scoped operations.

69. PROJECT ISOLATION
Lifecycle jobs shall always carry sufficient ownership context.
Minimum context:
organization_id
project_id
Workers must verify this context before executing destructive or archival operations.

70. DATA EXPORT
A project export shall preserve provenance.
A complete export may contain:
project metadata
requirements
model versions
revisions
geometry references
compliance manifests
validation results
drawings
schedules
document packages
audit records
provenance manifest
The export should include a machine-readable manifest.

71. EXPORT MANIFEST
Conceptually:
{
  "project_id": "uuid",
  "export_id": "uuid",
  "exported_at": "ISO-8601",
  "revision_ids": [],
  "artifact_ids": [],
  "content_hashes": {},
  "schema_version": "0.1"
}
The manifest permits verification of the exported dataset.

72. IMPORT PROVENANCE
Imported OneDraft project data shall not overwrite an existing project state without an explicit import operation.
The import process shall create an identifiable event:
PROJECT_IMPORT_STARTED
PROJECT_IMPORT_VALIDATED
PROJECT_IMPORT_COMPLETED
Imported objects shall retain their source provenance where possible.

73. SCHEMA VERSION PROVENANCE
Persistent exports and serialized project data shall identify the OneDraft schema version used to produce them.
Example:
schema_version = 0.1
Future migrations shall provide controlled conversion paths.

74. MIGRATION PROVENANCE
Database migrations shall be immutable and versioned.
Each deployment shall identify:
migration_version
migration_timestamp
application_version
The operational environment must therefore be able to determine which schema version produced a given project state.

75. SOFTWARE VERSION PROVENANCE
Finalized generated artifacts should, where appropriate, retain the software release that produced them.
Conceptually:
application_version
geometry_engine_version
documentation_engine_version
compliance_engine_version
aria_orchestration_version
This improves reproducibility of historical outputs.

76. DETERMINISTIC REGENERATION
Where deterministic regeneration is supported, the system should be able to reproduce a derived artifact from:
project state
revision
software version
configuration
template
code manifest
generation parameters
The resulting artifact can then be compared through content hashes.

77. NON-DETERMINISTIC AI OUTPUT
AI-generated operations may contain non-deterministic model behavior.
Therefore, OneDraft shall preserve the resulting command and resulting project state rather than relying solely on the ability to regenerate the exact AI response.
The authoritative record is the executed and validated project mutation.

78. PROVENANCE OF AI COMMANDS
An AI command record shall preserve:
command_id
project_id
revision_id
request_id
operation
target_objects
parameters
preserve_constraints
validation_requirements
result
status
created_at
completed_at
This record connects AI reasoning output to deterministic application execution.

79. DATA CONSISTENCY RULE
No derived artifact shall become authoritative merely because it exists in storage.
Authority follows the project model and version chain.
Conceptually:
MODEL STATE
    ↓
REVISION
    ↓
VALIDATED DERIVATION
    ↓
DOCUMENTATION
A file outside this chain is an external or unverified artifact.

80. ORPHAN ARTIFACT DETECTION
The system shall periodically identify artifacts that lack valid references to:
project
revision
model_version
generation job
Orphan artifacts may be:
quarantined
re-associated
archived
deleted
according to policy.

81. QUARANTINE
Potentially corrupt or untrusted artifacts may enter:
QUARANTINED
state.
A quarantined artifact shall not be presented as a final project deliverable.
Possible causes include:
hash mismatch
malformed file
unknown provenance
failed import
failed validation
security scan failure

82. SECURITY SCAN INTEGRATION
Imported artifacts may pass through the security subsystem before becoming active project data.
Conceptually:
UPLOAD
 ↓
QUARANTINE
 ↓
SECURITY CHECK
 ↓
HASH
 ↓
PROVENANCE
 ↓
ACTIVE
The security system remains responsible for the actual scanning policy.

83. DATA QUALITY STATES
Data may additionally carry quality state:
UNVERIFIED
VERIFIED
VALIDATED
FINAL
SUPERSEDED
QUARANTINED
Quality state and lifecycle state shall remain separate concepts.

84. DATA RETENTION METRICS
The system shall expose operational metrics including:
active_project_count
archived_project_count
artifact_count
artifact_storage_size
retention_candidates
deletion_candidates
quarantined_artifacts
orphan_artifacts
hash_verification_failures
provenance_failures
These metrics integrate with the Observability subsystem.

85. STORAGE OPTIMIZATION
Storage optimization may include:
deduplication
compression
tiered storage
artifact lifecycle policies
cache expiration
archive storage
Optimization shall never compromise authoritative project provenance.

86. DUPLICATE DETECTION
Content hashes may identify duplicate artifacts.
For example:
HASH A
 ↓
Artifact 1
Artifact 2
Artifact 3
The system may store a single physical object with multiple logical references where appropriate.
Logical ownership and provenance references must remain distinct.

87. ARTIFACT VERSIONING
Artifacts may be versioned independently from project revisions when required.
However, a final project artifact must always identify the project revision to which it belongs.
Example:
DRAWING ARTIFACT
 ↓
